@meta
id: 13-04
title: A range of characters
chapter: 13
type: lesson
skills: regex-classes
par: 32
minutes: 6
vscode: Ctrl+F with the regex toggle and the match-case toggle both on
vimrc: set ignorecase smartcase
open: order.provider.ts

@brief
Error codes are upper snake case, and the registry lookup is
case-sensitive. One `throw` in this provider was written with the code
in lower case, so at runtime it raises `UNKNOWN_ERROR` instead of the
one it names. Find it and upper-case the code.

There are eight `exception(` calls. Seven are fine. You are not going to
read them all — you are going to describe the broken one: an
`exception(` whose string starts with a *lower-case letter*.

`\d` gave you one built-in set. Square brackets let you build your own,
and a dash inside them means a range. One thing will get in your way:
this file runs with `ignorecase` and `smartcase`, the settings most
people use, and under `ignorecase` a range of lower-case letters matches
capitals too. Chapter 12 gave you the fix.

@keys
| `[a-z]` | any one lower-case letter |
| `[A-Z]`, `[0-9]`, `[0-9a-f]` | ranges, and ranges can be combined |
| `[aeiou]`, `[_-]` | a set of specific characters — a dash first or last is literal |
| `\C` | case matters for this pattern, whatever the settings say |
| `gUi'` | upper-case everything inside the quotes |

@hints
Describe the bad call: the function name, the bracket, the quote, and
then a letter from the wrong half of the alphabet.
--
`[a-z]` is "one lower-case letter". Under `ignorecase` it silently
becomes "one letter", so the search stops at the first `exception(`
call. `\C` at the front of the pattern puts case back.
--
`/\Cexception('[a-z]` `Enter` lands on the broken call. `gUi'`
upper-cases the string without you moving onto it.

@solution
/\Cexception('[a-z]<Enter>  gUi'  :wq<Enter>

Twenty-eight keystrokes, and the string was never selected or retyped.

A bracket expression matches exactly one character from the set inside
it. `[abc]` is one of `a`, `b` or `c`. `[a-z]` is a range; `[a-zA-Z_]`
is three things at once — two ranges and an underscore — which is a fair
description of "the first character of an identifier". `[0-9a-f]` is a
hex digit, which is how you describe a request id or a commit hash.

Most punctuation is ordinary inside brackets. `[.]` is a literal dot,
`[*]` a literal star. The characters that keep a meaning are `]`, `\`,
`^` at the very start and `-` between two others; put a dash first or
last (`[-+]`) when you mean the dash itself.

`\C` is the same `\C` you met in 12-10, doing the same job. It is worth
seeing it again here because bracket ranges are where `ignorecase`
bites hardest: `[A-Z]` under `ignorecase` matches every letter, and a
pattern that was written to tell the two cases apart quietly stops
doing so. Any time a range is doing the work, pin the case.

`gUi'` from chapter 8 and 10: the `gU` operator, the `i'` object. The
cursor was on the `e` of `exception`, two words short of the string, and
the quote object found it anyway — quote objects search forward along
the line for the first pair. That is a small thing to know and it saves
a `f'` on every one of these.

@start order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';
import { logger } from '../../infrastructure/logger';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async submit(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status !== 'DRAFT') {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_DRAFT');
    }

    if (order.lines.length === 0) {
      throw exception('ORDER_HAS_NO_LINES');
    }

    logger.info('order submitted', { orderId: order.id });

    return this.orderRepository.update({ where: { id }, values: { status: 'PENDING' } });
  }

  public async ship(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status !== 'PAID') {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_PAID');
    }

    logger.info('order shipped', { orderId: order.id });

    return this.orderRepository.update({ where: { id }, values: { status: 'SHIPPED' } });
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    if (order.status === 'CANCELLED') {
      throw exception('order_not_cancellable');
    }

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);

    logger.info('order cancelled', { orderId: order.id });

    return this.orderRepository.update({ where: { id }, values: { status: 'CANCELLED' } });
  }

  public async reopen(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status !== 'CANCELLED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_CANCELLED');
    }

    if (order.refundedAt) {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_REFUNDED');
    }

    return this.orderRepository.update({ where: { id }, values: { status: 'DRAFT' } });
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';
import { logger } from '../../infrastructure/logger';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async submit(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status !== 'DRAFT') {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_DRAFT');
    }

    if (order.lines.length === 0) {
      throw exception('ORDER_HAS_NO_LINES');
    }

    logger.info('order submitted', { orderId: order.id });

    return this.orderRepository.update({ where: { id }, values: { status: 'PENDING' } });
  }

  public async ship(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status !== 'PAID') {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_PAID');
    }

    logger.info('order shipped', { orderId: order.id });

    return this.orderRepository.update({ where: { id }, values: { status: 'SHIPPED' } });
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    if (order.status === 'CANCELLED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_CANCELLABLE');
    }

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);

    logger.info('order cancelled', { orderId: order.id });

    return this.orderRepository.update({ where: { id }, values: { status: 'CANCELLED' } });
  }

  public async reopen(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status !== 'CANCELLED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_CANCELLED');
    }

    if (order.refundedAt) {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_REFUNDED');
    }

    return this.orderRepository.update({ where: { id }, values: { status: 'DRAFT' } });
  }
}
